is is more flexible than committing to d4 immediately or developing the bishop before the knight.\n\nReason: Nf3 is the most principled developing move. It follows sound opening theory by developing a piece, controlling the center, and maintaining maximum flexibility. It's the foundation of many strong openings (Italian, Spanish, Scotch, etc.) and keeps all strategic options open while improving White's position.\n\nAnswer: N: g1-f3"}
