----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, R: e1-d1, R: e1-e2, R: e1-e3, R: e1-e4, R: e1-e5, R: e1-e6, R: e1-e7, R: e1-f1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, K: g1-f1, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation: Let me analyze this position. Black has developed their queen to d6 and bishop to g4, which are aggressive placements. Black's king is still on e8 (hasn't castled). White's king has moved to g1 and the rook is actively placed on e1. \n\nKey observations:\n1. Black's king is still in the center on e8 - this is a significant weakness\n2. My rook on e1 is perfectly placed on the open e-file, directly facing Black's uncastled king\n3. Black's queen on d6 and bishop on g4 are somewhat out of play for defending the king\n4. The move Re1-e7 would give check, forcing Black's king to move and winning the f7 pawn with a devastating attack\n\nLet me evaluate Re1-e7:\n- This gives check to the Black king on e8\n- Black must respond to the check (king must move or piece must block)\n- After Black moves the king (likely Kd8 or Kf8), I'll have won the f7 pawn and have a rook on the 7th rank, which is extremely powerful\n- This creates immediate tactical threats and prevents Black from castling\n\nAlternative moves like P: f2-f3 (attacking the bishop) or developing moves are slower and give Black time to castle and consolidate.\n\nReason: Re1-e7 delivers check while the Black king is stuck in the center, wins material (the f7 pawn), and places my rook on the dominant 7th rank. This creates immediate winning threats that Black cannot adequately defend against. The uncastled king position is Black's critical weakness, and this move exploits it maximally.\n\nAnswer: R: e1-e7"}
