----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, R: a1-c1, R: a1-d1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, B: e3-d2, B: e3-c1, B: e3-d4, B: e3-c5, B: e3-b6, B: e3-a7, B: e3-f4, B: e3-g5, B: e3-h6, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, K: g1-f1, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation: Let me analyze the position. I'm playing as White and the position is quite complex:\n\n**Material count:**\n- White: 1 Rook (a1), 1 Bishop (e3), 1 King (g1), 5 Pawns\n- Black: 2 Rooks (a8, h8), 1 Queen (b2!), 1 Bishop (e7), 1 Bishop (g4), 1 Knight (d1), 1 King (e8), 6 Pawns\n\nBlack has a significant material advantage with a Queen on b2 (very dangerous, close to my king) and a Knight on d1. This is a critical defensive position.\n\n**Key threats:**\n1. Black's Queen on b2 is extremely threatening, attacking my a1 Rook and near my King\n2. Black's Knight on d1 is also deep in my position\n3. My King on g1 has limited mobility\n\n**Promising moves to consider:**\n1. **B: e3-d2** - Blocks the Queen's attack on a1 Rook, defends, and attacks the Knight on d1\n2. **B: e3-c1** - Defends the a1 Rook from the Queen\n3. **K: g1-f1** or **K: g1-h1** - King safety moves\n4. **R: a1-b1** - Attacks the Queen, forcing it to move\n\n**Analysis:**\n- **B: e3-d2** is excellent: it blocks the Queen's diagonal to my Rook, attacks the Knight on d1, and centralizes my Bishop\n- **R: a1-b1** attacks the Queen but after Qxb1, I lose my Rook\n- King moves don't address the immediate tactical threats\n\nReason: B: e3-d2 is the strongest move because it simultaneously defends my Rook on a1 from the Queen's attack along the diagonal, attacks Black's Knight on d1, and improves my Bishop's position. This move addresses multiple threats at once and gains tempo by attacking the Knight.\n\nAnswer: B: e3-d2"}
